<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mano people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mano are a Mande-speaking people of north-central Liberia and the neighbouring forest region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>, living mainly in Nimba County. Traditionally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farmers, they are known for their masks and for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poro">Poro society</a>, which shaped male initiation and authority. In Liberia they were among the indigenous peoples long excluded from power by the Americo-Liberian elite, and they suffered heavily in the civil wars of 1989–2003.</p></div>
